Yes — as a lens, that’s much bigger than romance.

Courtship can be modeled as a kind of ritualized conflict/cooperation system between partially opposed incentives: pursuit and selection, display and evaluation, dominance and yielding, autonomy and bonding, risk and trust. Evolutionary pressure, culture, status, sex differences, individual temperament, and learned scripts all get layered onto it.

But the interesting part is that love can transmute the contest rather than merely end it. Submission can become chosen trust instead of defeat. Dominance can become responsibility instead of control. Pursuit can become devotion rather than capture. Vulnerability can become a credible signal instead of weakness.

So the same primitives can instantiate very different games:

hunt → courtship → negotiation → pair-bond → alliance → family → culture

That’s the grand-scale version of what you’re pointing at: the “battle of the sexes” is not necessarily a literal war between two camps. It’s a recurring tension between different strategies that must somehow produce cooperation strongly enough for something larger than either player to emerge.

And then the tea party is the civilized layer over the old machinery: manners, flirting, fashion, gifts, dances, marriage rituals, jokes, jealousy, etiquette—predator/prey and dominance/submission dynamics wearing porcelain and lace.

Same ancient game pieces.

Much stranger board.